//Working with software that improves everyday life and helps people thrive has always been a part of my journey as a software engineer. I am interested in how technology can be trusted and usefully integrated into people and the world, and I value the way that it makes a meaningful difference in people’s lives. That is why I chose to study at a university and pursue a Master’s degree with a focus on software engineering. My interest in building software that is reliable, flexible, and easy to use has deepened both my motivation and my commitment to the work. Through 5 years in international project work, I completed 5 projects with tangible outcomes, each of which reinforced the value of software that is sound, reliable, and easy to learn.

My interest in building software that is reliable, flexible, and scalable has been reinforced by the way I have worked with cross-platform tools and processes. For example, in a project requiring me to work with a tool that was outside my area of expertise, I put together a practical workaround that reduced the effort and time needed to complete the work. In another project, I needed to learn how to apply a new approach independently, and within 2 months I was using it productively in a real-world setting. In addition, colleagues have come to expect me to keep learning and apply new ideas independently, and I have been able to contribute by helping them do so. In one instance, a recurring problem was slowing the team down, so I put together a workaround that reduced the disruption and allowed the team to focus on the next step.

I am also interested in contributing as a Software Engineer. In a team, I can help build a practical, reliable, and scalable solution that is useful, easy to learn, and builds the foundation for a team of software engineers. I am also interested in contributing constructively in a team that is built on a foundation of shared values and shared purpose.

